-> submit_draft "1.0 p1 pattern filter json lines read from standard input => fact filter.input \"standard input\"\n1.0 p2 pattern keep a line only when every field named on the command line equals the value given with it => fact filter.match \"every named field equals its value\"\n1.0 p3 pattern print each kept line unchanged => fact filter.output \"each kept line unchanged\"\n1.0 p4 pattern install the tool as the command <name> => fact tool.command \"<name>\"\n1.0 p5 pattern given the lines <in1> and <in2> with <args>, print only <out> => fact witness.filter \"<in1> <in2> <args> <out>\"\n1.0 q1 demand filter.input \"what kind of lines are filtered, and where do they come from?\"\n1.0 q2 demand filter.match \"when should a line be kept?\"\n1.0 q3 demand filter.output \"what should happen to a kept line?\"\n1.0 q4 demand tool.command \"what should the installed command be called?\"\n1.0 q5 demand witness.filter \"give an example: which lines, which arguments, and what must be printed?\"\n1.0 r1 match fact filter.input => clause.logscan-main \"(define (logscan-main) (logscan-run (arguments)))\" ; clause.logscan-run \"(define (logscan-run spec) (let ((line (read-a-line))) (cond ((end-of-input? line) #t) (else (let ((shown (logscan-consider line spec))) (logscan-run spec))))))\"\n1.0 r2 match fact filter.match => clause.logscan-keep? \"(define (logscan-keep? record spec) (cond ((null? spec) #t) ((logscan-matches? record (car spec)) (logscan-keep? record (cdr spec))) (else #f)))\" ; clause.logscan-matches? \"(define (logscan-matches? record arg) (let ((cut (string-cut arg #\\\\=))) (cond ((not cut) (die \\\"argument is not field=value:\\\" arg)) (else (equal? (field-of record (field-name cut)) (field-value cut))))))\"\n1.0 r3 match fact filter.output => clause.logscan-consider \"(define (logscan-consider line spec) (let ((record (json-parse line))) (cond ((not record) #f) ((logscan-keep? record spec) (emit line)) (else #f))))\"\n1.0 r4 match fact tool.command => site.<self>.command \"\\\"<value>\\\"\" ; environment.systemPackages \"[ ${site} ]\"\n1.0 r5 match fact witness.filter => claim.filter.feed \"[ \\\"<value.1>\\\" \\\"<value.2>\\\" ]\" ; claim.filter.args \"[ \\\"<value.3>\\\" ]\" ; claim.filter.call \"(begin (logscan-main) (emitted))\" ; claim.filter.equals \"(list \\\"#<value.4>\\\")\"\n0.95 a1 expect site.<self>.command from tool.command\n0.9 a2 expect claim.filter.args from witness.filter#3\n0.9 a3 expect claim.filter.equals from witness.filter is \"(list \\\"<value.4>\\\")\"\n1.0 d1 report <<<lips\nThis is the `logscan` language: a handful of plain sentences describing a\nJSON line filter, realized as behaviour clauses (no source file) plus one\ninstalled command.\n\nThe line shapes it accepts, and what each means:\n\n- `filter JSON lines read from standard input.` -- fixes where input comes\n  from. It mints the entry point `logscan-main` and the read loop\n  `logscan-run`, which reads a line at a time until end of input.\n- `keep a line only when every field named on the command line equals the\n  value given with it.` -- the keep policy, `logscan-keep?` together with\n  `logscan-matches?`. Each command-line argument is cut at `=` into a field\n  name and a value; a line is kept only when every one of them matches the\n  parsed record. An argument that is not `field=value` stops the program\n  loudly, naming the offending argument.\n- `print each kept line unchanged.` -- `logscan-consider`: parse the line,\n  test it, and on a match emit the ORIGINAL text, not a re-serialization.\n  A line that is not JSON is silently skipped.\n- `install the tool as the command <name>.` -- the command name. The built\n  site is named after that word and put on the system PATH via\n  `environment.systemPackages`; no wrapper script is built around it.\n- `given the lines A and B with ARGS, print only OUT.` -- the author's\n  worked example. It becomes an offline claim: the two lines are fed to the\n  program, ARGS becomes its command